WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.330
(pensive music)

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:05.130
- [Adam] Cisco's revamped New York office

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.080
is packed with technology.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:08.610
There's tech that tracks.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.950
- There's 5,000 individual data points

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.410
that are being constantly
collected and harvested

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:14.550
in this facility.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.590
- And tech use for collaboration.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:17.760
Is there any space

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.320
in this office that doesn't have a camera?

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.340
- No, there isn't.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.500
- [Adam] Cisco's smart
office is both a showcase

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.690
of the company's technology and an example

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.910
of how it sees the workplace evolving.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.020
So how does it look?

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.720
And more importantly, how does it work?

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.310
I took a tour with the project's leaders

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.674
and popped into a few
meetings to find out.

00:00:34.674 --> 00:00:36.150
(meeting participants chuckling)

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.580
Cisco's 59,000 square foot office sits

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.950
in midtown Manhattan in One Penn Plaza,

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:44.370
exactly where it's been
for the last 17 years.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:45.720
But in the last two,

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.290
it's undergone an $18
million tech-driven redesign.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:50.550
- This is one of the few projects

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.770
where technology was really
at the very beginning

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:55.560
and pervasive throughout
the entire process.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.020
- In this space, the screens, cameras

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.500
and sensors came before
the lighting and furniture,

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.680
which was a new process
for both Gensler and Cisco.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.470
- We really think technology
is gonna be at the heart

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.690
of how people design
buildings moving forward.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.340
So we started it with that layer.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.420
- To see this tech,

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.430
all you have to do is look up.

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:17.790
The ceiling is littered with
tech, each of which doing more

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:18.990
than it might appear.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.213
So hard to miss this camera.

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.420
What is this camera doing?

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.430
- Actually, this was a
Meraki security camera

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.290
and obviously, it's
recording people coming in

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:26.123
and out of the floor,

00:01:26.123 --> 00:01:27.960
but it's also got a trip wire built in.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.370
We can actually count people

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:31.500
as they come in and
decrement as they leave.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:32.700
So at any given time,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.100
we know the overall
occupancy of the floor.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.660
- [Adam] But to know where people are

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.010
after they enter the office,

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:41.430
Cisco uses its wireless
access points like this one.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.250
- It's actually tracking
mobile devices as a proxy

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:46.560
for understanding occupancy
of space and people

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.120
but it's also tracking air quality,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.380
temperature, and humidity.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.197
- Is it tracking my
mobile device right now?

00:01:51.197 --> 00:01:53.070
- Yeah, it's tracking a mobile device.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.090
It doesn't know it's you

00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:56.250
but it knows there are
two mobile devices here

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.530
because I have one in my pocket as well.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.150
- Leave the hallway to enter a meeting

00:02:00.150 --> 00:02:02.970
and the cameras used to
connect employees on video call

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.700
then pick up the count, tracking
the number of participants

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.170
in the room.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.850
While employees may be anonymous,

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.950
this type of tracking isn't common.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.510
Just 1% of companies

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:16.380
in a 2022 Gartner poll have
sensors that track foot traffic.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.300
43% of companies say

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.090
they aren't tracking
onsite attendance at all.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.320
Are employees at all concerned

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.120
about the tracking that's possible

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.430
in an office where
cameras count the number

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.960
of bodies in the space?

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.550
- No, I don't believe they are.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.400
And the reason for that is at Cisco,

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.200
we don't have a set number

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.530
of days that we expect
employees to be in the office.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:41.430
I think if we had different
expectations around being

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.160
in the office, it may feel
a little bit different

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.410
but because we don't,
I think they understand

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.890
that those insights allow us
to make the space the best

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:51.000
that it can be for them.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.250
- [Adam] Still, these are Cisco products.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.770
Other companies that purchase
these systems could use them

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:59.310
for more specific tracking or
they could do as Cisco does

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:01.980
and feed the data here
for employees to see.

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.260
- Really what we have
here is the great ability

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:07.680
to show employees space availability.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.140
Red is obviously a space that is booked

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:13.590
and being used right now, or
Amber is a space that's booked

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:16.530
and not being used and
green is an available space.

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:17.790
- Let's find a space that we can go to.

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.640
- Yeah, so here's a space right here.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.020
- We'll go to this space.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:23.370
- Okay.

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:24.600
- Let's hold that.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.060
And that's gonna give us four
minutes to get over there.

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:29.040
- [Adam] Four minutes
because in this office,

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.900
space is meant to be shared.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.200
So rooms like the ones lining
this hall aren't bookable.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.420
The red and green lights are your signal.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:38.640
- See, this is our room
that we're gonna use.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:39.473
Yep.

00:03:39.473 --> 00:03:40.306
- All right.

00:03:40.306 --> 00:03:42.090
These ad hoc spaces represent one

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:46.500
of the biggest changes to
Cisco's space: how it's balanced.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.090
Take a look at Cisco's floor plan.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:51.750
These spaces are what Cisco
considers individual workspaces.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:53.970
These spaces are for teams.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:57.840
- In the past, our building
was set up where 70%

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.960
of the space was targeted
toward individuals

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:03.270
and 30% of the space was
targeted toward teams.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:04.560
And we flipped that.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.110
- [Adam] That resulted in
the 90 collaboration spaces

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.740
seen here and left just
50 individual desks.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:13.320
- I think this is a new era and I think

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.890
that when you want to sit by
yourself and get work done,

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:19.230
you may not choose the office to do that.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.750
- [Adam] There are actually
about 1,700 employees assigned

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.760
to this office but Cisco doesn't expect

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.740
that they'll all be in on the same day.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:26.910
- With hybrid here to stay,

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.560
we're seeing so much more emphasis

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.380
on amenity spaces, collaboration spaces.

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:34.320
People are coming in to
be with other people.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.660
The things that they can't do
when they're working at home.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.520
- [Adam] Things like
brainstorming on a whiteboard

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.130
or meeting in a 14-person
room like this one

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.330
are now the focus.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:43.320
- So what's really interesting

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.630
about this space is how the space design,

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:48.420
how the furnishings and the
technology all come together.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:49.253
- [Adam] They come together

00:04:49.253 --> 00:04:51.840
because Cisco designed the
table to work specifically

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.400
with the camera at the other end,

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.250
from the placement of the
microphones to this matte finish,

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:59.310
which helps reflect light up
onto the faces of the table.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:02.070
Most noticeable though is
the table's tapered shape.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.980
- We shape the table to make
sure everybody has a line

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:08.610
of sight on the display
and can see content.

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:09.810
And we also make sure

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.470
that people that are
remote that are joining us

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.080
actually have line of sight
of everybody at the table.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.540
- Right, because that person
all the way at the end

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.940
is much further to the right than I am.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.010
So they should be able
to see every single face

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:23.970
down the road.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.100
That shape and the exclusion of chairs

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.020
on the screen side is pervasive

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.050
in Cisco's space because it says 98%

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.480
of its meetings will have
a remote participant.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.040
So who is this conference
room designed for?

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:36.810
Is it designed for the people who are here

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:38.400
at this table or is it designed

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.230
for the people who are
signing in remotely?

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:42.810
- When we think about
designing collaboration spaces

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:44.790
like this, we spend as much time

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.700
if not more about worrying
about the remote participant.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.880
We wanna make sure there's
absolute digital equality.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.260
- [Adam] Digital equality,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.150
that's an idea that Cisco stressed

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.820
and something it thinks
its hybrid workforce needs.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:05:59.610
- Something that we have all experienced

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:01.170
is sometimes you're in a meeting

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:03.120
and you're having a hard time breaking in

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.090
and especially if you
happen to be the only one

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:09.210
in the office or the only
one working remotely.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.460
- And here's where the
camera can help bring people

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:13.770
or at least their faces closer.

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:15.420
I tested it with colleagues who work

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:17.010
in two separate offices.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:19.530
Are you seeing this full room?

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:21.130
Are you seeing this whole table?

00:06:21.970 --> 00:06:23.470
- No, I'm seeing four chairs.

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:25.800
- Same. I just sent you a screen grab.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.030
- Oh, okay.
- Yeah.

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:30.633
- So you're really only seeing
like my side of the room.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:34.560
There is an argument to
be made that with all

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.790
of Cisco's technology and the
ability to make every space

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.310
into a hybrid meeting
space that the office

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:42.960
isn't really needed at all.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:43.890
What would you say to that?

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:46.290
- Well, I think that
at the end of the day,

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.120
people will want to come together

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:50.580
but they're gonna come
together for different reasons.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.560
- But for those who'd rather stay home

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.450
the tech is here to support that.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:57.750
And that's an example that
other companies may follow.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.910
So do you expect other
companies you work with

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:02.670
to want to do tech first, design second?

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:04.770
- Absolutely, I mean, so many
of our clients are interested

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:07.080
in seeing this space because
of how this was built.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:09.210
I think anyone that's
looking to build a new space

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:10.481
is gonna think this way.

00:07:10.481 --> 00:07:13.148
(pensive music)

